Consider a coherent polycrystalline solid in which the local crystallographic state is described by two coupled fields: a positional/density order parameter ψ(x,t) encoding the periodic crystalline pattern, and an orientation field θ(x,t) (taking values in SO(2) in two dimensions, or SO(3) in three dimensions) representing the local rotation of the crystal lattice. In a coherent crystal, the lattice is connected throughout the body, so spatial gradients of θ correspond to geometrically necessary dislocations, whose Nye dislocation density tensor α can be expressed in terms of derivatives of θ and of the elastic distortion.

Standard phase-field models of polycrystalline solidification postulate a purely local relaxational dynamics for the orientation field, schematically of the form
   ∂θ/∂t = − M_θ · δF/δθ,
where F is a free-energy functional and M_θ is a (possibly orientation- and ψ-dependent) mobility. Such an equation does not, in general, respect the kinematic constraint that, in a coherent crystal, changes of θ are produced by the motion of dislocations and must be consistent with the evolution of the dislocation density α, with elastic interactions among dislocations, with externally applied strain, and with interactions with other defects (grain boundaries, free surfaces, second-phase particles, etc.).

Problem. Formulate a tractable, physically consistent evolution equation for the orientation field θ(x,t) in a coherent polycrystalline body, compatible with a phase-field (or phase-field-crystal) description of the underlying density field, such that:
 (i) ∂θ/∂t is derived from, and consistent with, the kinematic evolution of the Nye dislocation density tensor α[θ, u] (with u the elastic displacement);
 (ii) the resulting dynamics correctly accounts for the elastic interactions between dislocations and for an applied external strain field;
 (iii) it incorporates the interaction of dislocations with other defects present in the system;
 (iv) it reduces to known limits (e.g. grain-boundary motion by coupled shear/migration, recovery, polygonization) in appropriate regimes;
 (v) it is computationally tractable at the mesoscale, in the sense that it can be coupled to standard phase-field equations for the density and concentration fields without prohibitive cost.

Provide such an equation, or prove that, under stated physical hypotheses, no equation satisfying (i)–(v) exists.

